# Copy to .env.local and fill in what you need. Everything here is optional
# for local work: with none of it set the app runs the demo end to end on
# the file store, a deterministic segmentation fallback, and a demo
# geocoder pin.
#
# A REAL DEPLOYMENT is a different list — AUTH_SECRET and DATABASE_URL are
# required, the four S3_* variables are all-or-nothing, and shipping
# without ANTHROPIC_API_KEY means shipping the labelled demo overlay to
# customers. docs/deploy.md is the runbook and says which is which.

# --- Database (project-map section 3) --------------------------------------
# Postgres. Set this and lib/store runs on Drizzle; leave it unset and
# projects live as JSON under .data/. After setting it, run:
#   npm run db:setup     # migrate, then seed the org's price book
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host:5432/landscapeai
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=5

# Schema to run in. Only useful for isolating an environment inside one
# database; the test suite sets it to a disposable schema per run.
# DB_SCHEMA=public

# Postgres compiled to wasm and run in-process, as an alternative to a
# server. "memory://" is disposable; a path persists. Ignored when
# DATABASE_URL is set.
# PGLITE_DATA_DIR=memory://

# Where the file store keeps projects when no database is configured.
# PROJECTS_DATA_DIR=./.data/projects

# --- Rate limiting (see lib/ratelimit, docs/deploy.md) ---------------------
# On by default. Per-IP token buckets at the edge of the app, with the
# tightest budgets on the two anonymous routes that spend real money:
# POST /api/projects (25 MB in, ~1.5 s of CPU-bound image decode) and
# POST /api/vision (one Anthropic call). Budgets are in
# lib/ratelimit/policy.ts, not here — they are code, with tests.
#
# The header the platform writes the caller's real address into. Leave it
# unset and the limiter tries fly-client-ip, cf-connecting-ip,
# true-client-ip, x-real-ip and finally x-forwarded-for. SET IT in
# production: a header the client can forge is a budget the client can
# reset.
# RATE_LIMIT_CLIENT_IP_HEADER=fly-client-ip
#
# "off" disables the limiter. For local development and for npm run shots,
# never for a deployment.
# RATE_LIMIT=off

# --- Site metadata ---------------------------------------------------------
# Absolute base for Open Graph and icon URLs in app/layout.tsx. Only matters
# once links to this app are shared somewhere that unfurls them; without it
# the metadata points at localhost.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://landscape.example.com

# --- Contractor auth (project-map section 3) -------------------------------
# Signs contractor session cookies. REQUIRED in production — without it the
# app refuses to serve, because a predictable secret means anyone can mint a
# session and the console shows cost, margin and every lead's contact
# details. Generate one with: openssl rand -base64 32
# AUTH_SECRET=

# With a database, contractor logins are rows: npm run db:user
# Without one there is no user table, so a single login can be configured
# here. Leave these unset and nobody can sign in — which is the safe
# default, not a broken one. The customer side never signs in either way.
# CONTRACTOR_EMAIL=
# CONTRACTOR_PASSWORD=
# CONTRACTOR_NAME=

# --- Vision (Phase 2) ------------------------------------------------------
# Without a key, segmentation falls back to a clearly-labeled demo overlay.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# --- Photo storage (project-map section 3) ---------------------------------
# S3-compatible object storage for photos (R2, Supabase Storage, MinIO, S3).
# Set ALL FOUR of these or none of them: half-configured is a startup-time
# error, not a quiet fall back to keeping photos in this deployment.
#
# With none set, photo bytes stay where they were — a photo_objects row when
# DATABASE_URL is set, a file under .data/ when it is not — so a clean
# checkout still runs the demo with nothing to provision.
#
# The bucket must be PRIVATE. Nothing hands a browser an object URL: reads
# go through the app, which is where the access rule lives (see
# lib/storage/index.ts, "Who may read a photo").
# S3_BUCKET=landscape-photos
# S3_ENDPOINT=https://<account>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# S3_REGION=auto
# S3_PREFIX=                  # optional key prefix, for a shared bucket
# S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true    # false for virtual-hosted-style buckets

# --- Imagery and geocoding (Phase 3) ---------------------------------------
# THESE TWO DECLARATIONS GATE THE AERIAL LEG. `/design/[projectId]/locate`,
# POST /api/geocode and the drawing routes are served only when BOTH say
# "permitted" — see lib/locate/gate.ts. Anything else, including unset, is
# "unreviewed", which is off, and the rest of the funnel runs without it:
# the customer keeps their design and a typology band. Declaring a licence
# is the operator's act; nothing in this repo assumes one.
#
# Both are NEXT_PUBLIC_, which means INLINED AT BUILD TIME. Changing either
# needs a rebuild, not a restart, and a container build needs them passed as
# --build-arg as well as set at runtime (see Dockerfile).
#
# GEOCODER=demo
# Satellite tiles, behind lib/imagery/provider.ts. Left unset, the map draws
# Esri World Imagery, which is a DEMO tile set: the licence has not been
# reviewed for deriving and selling measurements (project-map section 3 —
# the open decision). Declare the term you have actually licensed.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SATELLITE_TILE_URL=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SATELLITE_ATTRIBUTION=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SATELLITE_MAX_ZOOM=19
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SATELLITE_LICENCE=unreviewed   # unreviewed | prohibited | permitted
#
# The geocoder's commercial terms. Nominatim (the default, keyless) does
# NOT cover production commercial traffic under its usage policy; Mapbox
# and Google are the paid candidates project-map section 3 names. Declare
# the terms you have actually signed.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GEOCODER_LICENCE=unreviewed    # unreviewed | prohibited | permitted
